Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:28:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Control channel: every request needs the window's key

Any local program, and possibly a web page, can reach a loopback port, and
GridCraft's control channel ran any command for anyone who connected.
This brings over WordCraft's key scheme (docs/control-protocol.md, Keys):

- gridcraft-control-key (new, L0): the settings folder and the key file's
  name, control-key.local-<port> (or the Flatpak instance id), shared by
  the app and the MCP bridge. The app's config_dir is now this folder.
- gridcraft: once the port is bound, the control server writes a random
  256-bit key (getrandom) to the key file (mode 0600 on Unix, a stale
  file replaced) and the window removes it on exit. Every request needs
  the key, as top-level `key` or once per connection with `auth`
  (compared in constant time); a missing or wrong key gets
  `unauthorized` and the connection is closed. A connection without an
  authorised request is closed after 30 s (one deadline: blank lines and
  bad JSON don't extend it); a line that isn't JSON closes the connection
  (an HTTP request can't smuggle a command); a line over 64 MiB is
  refused and closes it. Without a settings folder or key file, requests
  are refused.
- mcp: Remote (`gridcraft-cli mcp --connect`, `send`) takes the key from
  GRIDCRAFT_CONTROL_KEY or the key file for the port, reads it again on
  every new connection (and once more after an `unauthorized`, for an
  app that restarted), and sends it only to loopback addresses, with a
  clear error otherwise.
- docs: control-protocol.md Keys; mcp.md.

Tests ported from WordCraft for the server (refusals, auth, deadline,
line limit, HTTP smuggling, key file life), the key file name and the
bridge (env then file, loopback only, re-read after a restart).

